Subject: Do not build a stack trace for thrown values that cannot carry one

What this does
  Collects stack frames on the throw path only when the value being thrown can
  hold a trace, instead of also collecting them to fill the context's
  error-backtrace slot.

Why we need it
  Frames were collected before it was known whether the value could carry them,
  so a thrown primitive, plain object or thenable paid for a complete stack walk
  with string formatting, on a path that programs use for control flow. Only a
  reporter that reads the slot through js_std_cmd(ErrorBackTrace=2, ...) could
  observe it, and nothing in this runtime does.

What it adds
  One condition with one clause fewer: `needs_backtrace()` decides on its own.
  An Error is unaffected -- its stack is still captured when it is constructed,
  still readable, and still names the throwing function after a rethrow. Every
  other value propagates untouched, with its identity, prototype and type
  preserved. No field is added to the runtime, and no opcode is added or retired;
  BC_VERSION is unchanged.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -24294,8 +24294,7 @@ static JSValue JS_CallInternal(JSContext *caller_ctx, JSValueConst func_obj,
         }
     }
  exception:
-    if (needs_backtrace(rt->current_exception)
-    || JS_IsUndefined(ctx->error_back_trace)) {
+    if (needs_backtrace(rt->current_exception)) {
         sf->cur_pc = pc;
         build_backtrace(ctx, rt->current_exception, JS_UNDEFINED,
                         NULL, 0, 0, 0);
